// User-confirmed scope, 12 October 2026. No invented prices, products or project evidence.
const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
const p=s=>`<p>${esc(s)}</p>`;
const link=(r,s)=>`<a href="/${r}/">${esc(s)}</a>`;
const topic=(id,route,q,body,a)=>({id,route,q,body,a});
export const educationalTopics=[
 topic('Q001','roof-leak-repairs','Why does my roof only leak during heavy rain?','A leak that appears only in heavy rain can involve the amount of water reaching a junction or drainage route. Record whether rain intensity, duration or wind changes the pattern. The roof covering, flashings and outlets need assessment together; dry conditions between storms do not show that the defect has disappeared.','Heavy rain can reveal a water-entry or drainage problem not apparent in lighter rain. Note the timing and affected room so the connected roof and drainage can be assessed.'),
 topic('Q002','roof-leak-repairs','Why does my roof leak when it is windy?','Wind-driven rain can reach connections differently from rain falling in calm conditions. A leak during wind is a useful observation, not proof of a particular flashing or tile defect. Include the affected room and any wind direction you already know; there is no need to go outside in bad weather to investigate.','Wind can change how rain reaches roof connections. An inspection needs to relate the weather pattern to the covering, laps and flashings rather than assuming one cause.'),
 topic('Q003','roof-leak-repairs','What causes ceiling stains after rain?','A bedroom ceiling stain appearing after the first heavy rain is a reason to investigate water entry, even if no drip is visible. Note when the mark first appeared and whether it grows. Roof defects are one possible source; plumbing, equipment and condensation may also be relevant. A photograph cannot identify the cause by itself.','A rain-related stain may indicate water entry, but the ceiling mark alone cannot identify the source. Record when it appears and arrange an assessment of the relevant roof area.'),
 topic('Q004','roof-leak-repairs','Does a ceiling stain always mean the roof is leaking?','A ceiling stain records moisture at the visible surface, not necessarily a hole immediately above it. Explain whether it appears in dry weather, near plumbing or when equipment runs. These observations help direct the investigation instead of automatically booking roof sealing or painting over the mark.','No. Roof water entry, plumbing, equipment or condensation can be relevant. Timing and location help direct the assessment; a stain is not a diagnosis.'),
 topic('Q005','roof-leak-repairs','Can water travel before appearing on the ceiling?','Water can move along connected materials before reaching an interior lining. A stain may therefore be below a different part of the roof from the entry point. Describe both the indoor location and any safely visible external feature, but leave close-up tracing and roof-space access to the assessment.','Yes. Water may travel along connected materials before becoming visible. The inspection should consider the water path, not only the spot directly above the stain.'),
 topic('Q006','roof-leak-repairs','Why is the visible stain away from the damaged roof area?','Roof slopes, junctions and supporting materials can separate the point of entry from the point where water becomes visible. Marking the room and approximate position helps the inspection connect observations. Do not assume a distant rust mark or broken tile explains the stain without checking the route between them.','The entry point and visible stain can differ because water follows connected surfaces. The route needs assessment before a particular roof defect is blamed.'),
 topic('Q007','roof-leak-repairs','Why is my roof still leaking after a repair?','A returning leak needs the earlier scope reviewed alongside the current weather pattern and roof condition. Share which components were worked on, when the leak returned and any existing records. The original source may remain, another connection may be involved, or conditions may have changed; repeating the same patch does not identify the source.','A recurring leak can involve an unresolved source, a connected defect or changed conditions. Provide the previous work records and new leak timing before another repair is specified.'),
 topic('Q008','roof-leak-repairs','Why has roof sealant not stopped the leak?','Sealant at a visible joint does not establish that the full water path has been corrected. The surrounding material, overlaps and drainage may need different work. If the same area leaks after sealing, discuss investigation of those connections before adding more sealant or treating the roof surface.','Sealant cannot substitute for identifying the source and assessing adjoining components. A recurring leak needs investigation before another surface patch.'),
 topic('Q009','tile-roof-repairs','Can a cracked roof tile cause a leak?','A cracked, broken or missing tile may allow rain past the covering, depending on the position and surrounding roof detail. It should be assessed with the overlaps and adjoining components. A crack is relevant evidence, but it does not by itself explain every ceiling mark or establish that the whole roof needs restoration.','A cracked tile can allow water entry, but its position and connected details matter. Inspect the affected tiles and adjoining roof before setting the repair scope.'),
 topic('Q010','tile-roof-repairs','Can individual roof tiles be replaced?','Local replacement can suit a few damaged concrete or terracotta tiles where the surrounding roof remains serviceable. An older tile needs a compatible profile and overlap, not merely a similar colour. Spare tiles and supplier availability can help establish a match; availability is checked rather than guaranteed in advance.','Yes, where the surrounding roof and matching material support a local repair. The tile profile, fit and availability need checking before replacement is confirmed.'),
 topic('Q011','ridge-capping-repointing','What is the difference between roof repointing and rebedding?','Repointing renews the exposed pointing at ridge connections; rebedding addresses the bedding that supports the ridge tiles. Crumbling surface material and loose or unsupported capping are different observations. Inspection establishes whether pointing work is suitable or whether the bedding also needs attention before the scope is quoted.','Repointing addresses exposed pointing, while rebedding renews the supporting bed. The stability and condition of the ridge determine which work is needed.'),
 topic('Q012','ridge-capping-repointing','How do I know if ridge capping needs repair?','Cracked or missing mortar, displaced ridge tiles and recurring water near a ridge are reasons to request an assessment. Do not test a ridge tile by hand or walk on the roof. The inspection checks the support, pointing and adjoining tiles to distinguish a local surface issue from a wider ridge repair.','Visible cracks, missing mortar or displaced ridge tiles warrant assessment. The condition of the support and surrounding tiles determines the repair, not the crack alone.'),
 topic('Q013','flashing-repairs','What is a roof valley?','A roof valley is the channel where adjoining roof slopes meet and direct rainwater towards the lower drainage route. On many tiled roofs, formed metal carries water between the slopes. Debris, tile edges and the condition of the channel all matter when assessing a leak or maintenance requirement.','A valley directs water where roof slopes meet. Its channel, adjoining tiles and downstream drainage need to be considered together.'),
 topic('Q014','flashing-repairs','When does valley iron need replacing?','Rust on a valley needs assessment of the metal condition and the adjoining roof, rather than an automatic replacement decision. Deposits may need removal, while deterioration through the material may require replacement of an affected run. Ask which connections and tile work are included and how the downstream drainage is addressed.','Replacement may be appropriate where deterioration means the valley material cannot support a suitable repair. Cleaning alone does not repair damaged metal.'),
 topic('Q015','flashing-repairs','What does roof flashing do?','Roof flashing helps shed water at roof-to-wall connections, chimneys, skylights and other junctions or penetrations. Its form and relationship to adjoining materials matter as much as the visible edge. A leaking wall junction needs those connected details considered, rather than assuming that adding sealant to the surface will resolve it.','Flashing manages water where roof materials meet another surface or opening. The formed detail and adjoining connections work together.'),
 topic('Q016','flashing-repairs','What are the signs of damaged roof flashing?','Separation, lifted edges, corrosion and recurring staining near a junction are reasons for a closer assessment. A stain beside a chimney, patio connection or wall does not prove the flashing is the sole source. Describe the location and timing so the surrounding roof and possible alternative sources can be checked.','Lifted or separated edges, corrosion and rain-related stains can indicate a flashing problem. The junction and surrounding roof need inspection to establish the cause.'),
 topic('Q017','metal-roof-repairs','Can roof screws and washers cause leaks?','A roof fixing relies on the fastener, sealing washer and the surrounding sheet working together. A weathered washer or loose fixing can be relevant, but corrosion at the hole can change the repair required. Roof screw replacement and washer work are specified from the condition of the fixing point, not from the screw alone.','Yes. Fixings, washers and the surrounding metal can contribute to water entry. Assess the whole fixing point before deciding whether replacement alone is suitable.'),
 topic('Q018','metal-roof-repairs','Can a metal roof be repaired without replacing every sheet?','Targeted repair may suit damaged screws, flashings or individual sheets where adjoining roofing remains serviceable. Tin, corrugated iron and COLORBOND roof descriptions still require the actual profile and finish to be identified. Loose sheets rattling in wind need assessment of fixings and surrounding material, not an assumption that the entire roof must be replaced.','Often a local scope can be considered, depending on the sheet and connected components. Widespread deterioration may call for a broader assessment.'),
 topic('Q019','flashing-repairs','Is a leaking skylight caused by the skylight or the roof around it?','A water mark near a skylight can relate to its surrounding flashing, adjoining roofing or the skylight itself. Roof repair scope must distinguish those parts from glazing or device work. The same distinction applies around vents, whirlybirds and air-conditioning penetrations: roof connections and equipment faults are not interchangeable tasks.','Either may be relevant. The roof junction and the skylight need to be distinguished before the work is agreed; a roof repair is not automatically skylight replacement.'),
 topic('Q020','gutter-repairs','Can overflowing gutters cause water to enter the roof space?','Overflow can allow water towards adjoining eaves or building junctions, depending on the arrangement. Inspect the gutter run, outlet and connected downpipe rather than assuming a visible overflow is just leaves. A leak that returns after clearing may involve fall, damaged material or a connected drainage restriction.','Overflow can affect adjoining building details. The gutter, outlet and downpipe need a connected assessment; clearing alone may not resolve a recurring problem.'),
 topic('Q021','box-gutter-repairs','What causes a box gutter to overflow?','Debris, restricted outlets, damaged gutter material or a drainage arrangement unable to manage the water reaching it can contribute to box gutter overflow. Because an internal gutter sits within connected building details, its outlets and overflow route need assessment with the surrounding roof. We undertake box gutter repairs in Perth and define the affected components and access in the quote.','Blockages, outlet restrictions, deterioration or drainage limitations can be relevant. Assess the box gutter and its connected outlets and overflow route before choosing the repair.'),
 topic('Q022','roof-leak-repairs','Is it a roof leak, condensation or a plumbing leak?','Moisture in dry weather, dampness associated with equipment and rain-related water entry need different observations. Condensation occurs when moisture in the air condenses on a cooler surface, so it is not the same mechanism as rain entering a roof defect. Explain the timing; the appropriate assessment may involve another trade rather than roof sealing.','Timing helps direct investigation but is not conclusive. Roof water entry, condensation and plumbing or equipment issues need distinguishing before a repair is prescribed.'),
 topic('Q023','repair-options','What is the difference between roof repair and roof restoration?','A repair addresses a defined defect or component. Restoration coordinates suitable component repairs with an agreed preparation and finishing scope. A few broken tiles or an isolated leak do not automatically require whole-roof cleaning and painting. Compare the actual included work before deciding which approach addresses your objective.','Repair targets a defect. Restoration combines suitable repairs with agreed preparation or finishing; it is not automatically necessary for a local leak.'),
 topic('Q024','roof-painting','Does roof painting fix roof leaks?','Painting is a surface-finishing stage, not a substitute for identifying water entry. Our sequence is cleaning before spray painting, with required defect repairs and surface suitability established as part of the agreed scope. Confirm the preparation and product system for the actual roof; a fresh appearance does not prove a leak has been corrected.','Painting alone does not establish that a roof leak has been repaired. Identify and correct the relevant defects before the agreed cleaning, preparation and spray-painting stages.'),
 topic('Q025','roof-replacement','When is roof replacement more suitable than repair?','Widespread deterioration or surrounding material that cannot support an appropriate local repair may make replacement worth considering. Age or appearance alone is not enough. We undertake whole-roof replacement in Perth; the assessment compares what can be retained, the extent of removal and the proposed new covering before the replacement scope is agreed.','Replacement may be more suitable when the roof cannot support an appropriate repair. Ask for the findings and a clear comparison of retained and replaced components.'),
 topic('Q026','roof-inspection','Is a second roof assessment worthwhile?','A second assessment can be useful when the cause remains unclear, leaks recur or competing quotes propose very different work. Share the earlier findings and repair history, and ask what the new assessment will examine. It should clarify evidence and limitations, not simply promise that another patch will work.','It can help clarify an unexplained recurring leak or different repair proposals. Agree the assessment scope and fee, and provide earlier records.'),
 topic('Q027','roof-inspection','What should a roof inspection include?','Agree the purpose before booking: active leak investigation, roof condition or planned maintenance. Relevant coverings, ridges, flashings, penetrations and drainage should be considered within the accessible scope. We charge for inspections; confirm the fee and which findings, photographs or written records can be supplied before booking, particularly for a sale or insurer request.','Confirm the purpose, accessible components, limitations, inspection fee and available records. A roof assessment is not automatically a whole-building, engineering or insurance report.'),
 topic('Q028','roof-repairs','What should I look for in a roof repair quote?','Compare the same repair items rather than the totals alone. The quote should make clear the affected components, materials, access, preparation, waste removal and exclusions, with optional surface work separated. Ask how hidden deterioration or extra work will be agreed and request the terms applicable to the particular scope.','Check the repair items, materials, access, preparation, waste removal, exclusions and approval of additional work. Confirm inspection charges and applicable written terms separately.'),
 topic('Q029','roof-maintenance','How should I prepare my roof for Perth’s winter rain?','Before winter rain, gather existing leak history and note visible changes from ground level. Arrange an assessment of the covering, ridge and flashing connections, valleys, gutters and outlets where relevant. Prioritise damaged components and recurring overflow rather than relying on cosmetic work.','Record previous leaks and arrange relevant roof and drainage checks before winter. Stay at ground level; the property condition determines maintenance priorities.'),
 topic('Q030','roof-leak-repairs','What information should I send when reporting a roof leak?','Send the suburb, affected room or roof area, when water appears, roof material if known and previous work details. Mention solar installation, nearby equipment or changes preceding the leak without assuming causation. Existing safe photos are optional. For rentals, include the authorised owner or manager and access contact; do not climb onto the roof to gather evidence.','Include the location, timing, affected area, known roof material and previous repairs. Existing safe photographs are optional; identify the person authorised to arrange access.')
];

